{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'apply_patch() { /usr/local/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/node_modules/@openai/codex-linux-x64/vendor/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/bin/codex --codex-run-as-apply-patch \"$@\"; }\napply_patch \"$(cat <<'\"'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /workspace/paper/paper_strategy.json\n+{\n+  \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\",\n+  \\\"selected_prompt_family\\\": \\\"paper_standalone\\\",\n+  \\\"recommended_title\\\": \\\"Endpoint-Tuned Regression under Network Interference: Accuracy and Limits\\\",\n+  \\\"central_question\\\": \\\"How accurately does endpoint-risk-tuned one-hop regression estimate the global average treatment effect across the evaluated networks, interference mechanisms, and assignment designs, relative to simple regression baselines?\\\",\n+  \\\"central_thesis\\\": \\\"In the evaluated synthetic-outcome settings, endpoint-tuned exposure regression reduces error relative to assignment-only comparators in many spillover conditions but performs worse in aggregate than linear one-hop OLS, while near-noise-level in-sample residuals coexist with substantial endpoint errors under threshold and omitted two-hop interference.\\\",\n+  \\\"narrative_rationale\\\": \\\"The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation, not the development score: final.evidence.episodes contains 24 seed blocks, each with four graph families and six scientific conditions, permitting direct matched comparisons of the frozen estimator with recorded baselines. The scientifically useful result is conditional accuracy and failure, including an unfavorable aggregate comparison with one-hop OLS. The four graph families belong to one shared question, with family-specific evidence rather than separate chronological experiments. Endpoint-targeted tuning is a concrete implemented method, but neither its isolated benefit nor the cause of every error is established. A universal-improvement story, a broad architecture comparison, and a search-progression story are weaker because component ablations and valid final results for those alternative families are absent.\\\",\n+  \\\"prior_paper_story_status\\\": \\\"recentered\\\",\n+  \\\"edit_framing_decision\\\": \\\"This is a fresh framing, not a manuscript-edit run: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper, aggregated, extra_tasks, or contributions scaffold is present. Recentered denotes moving from the source task's broad accuracy agenda to the supported endpoint-regression comparison and its limitations; it does not assert that a prior manuscript was revised. Preserving a hypothetical prior story would invent evidence. Broadening to a shared estimator/architecture family is unsupported. Centering the completed final comparison is preferable to either broadening or a development-improvement narrative.\\\",\n+  \\\"staged_input_impact\\\": [\n+    \\\"The inspected final-evaluated method's available source is results/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1/src/program.py, linked by paper_data.best_correct and results/best_program_manifest.json. It implements quadratic one-hop exposure features, endpoint-risk ridge selection, estimability checks, and fixed-hinge fallback; it is not a neural or implemented two-hop estimator. The final manifest records a frozen program-tree hash, but this strategy does not claim an independent file-by-file hash verification.\\\",\n+    \\\"The original implementation at task/program_0/src/program.py uses fixed one-hop hinges and fixed ridge regularization. Its development evidence is not a matched final comparison with the frozen estimator. Omit an original-versus-selected final contrast rather than mixing search and final results; the directly recorded final one-hop OLS comparator remains essential.\\\",\n+    \\\"paper_data.final_evaluation was read independently: final.qualification.status is completed, final.qualification.outcome is qualified, and final.qualification.correct is true. These reported checks do not establish superiority, model adequacy, generalization, or publication readiness. No downstream_study or study.* grounding sources exist in this bundle.\\\",\n+    \\\"The final records, including Stanford3 and the unfavorable comparisons, change the center of gravity from optimizing a development score to mapping conditional endpoint error. The empty figures directory and absent ablation_studies require new visualizations of existing records, not invented experiments.\\\",\n+    \\\"The two-hop GCV branch has a failed evaluation artifact and no usable performance result. Its stderr reports a disconnection before receiving candidate responses; the underlying cause and estimator performance remain unknown. It is missing evidence, not a measured loss or evidence against two-hop adjustment. No externally staged new/old task families or prior manuscript were supplied.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"method_groups\\\": [\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Endpoint-risk-tuned quadratic one-hop regression\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"proposed\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The frozen final-evaluated method: covariates, own treatment, neighbor treatment fraction, treatment-exposure interaction, exposure curvature, and treatment-curvature interaction; centered/scaled SVD ridge with a fixed penalty grid and endpoint-contrast risk criterion. Explain guards, constant-outcome bypass, and fixed-hinge fallback without claiming their observed frequencies.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Difference in means\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"A recorded final comparator that ignores interference and provides an interpretable reference for direct-treatment contrasts versus the global intervention target. Retain its favorable no-spillover results.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Own-treatment OLS with covariates\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"A recorded final comparator separating ordinary covariate adjustment from explicit exposure adjustment. Its similarity to difference in means is informative rather than grounds for silently dropping it.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Linear one-hop OLS with covariates\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The strongest recorded aggregate final comparator and the central negative control against a universal tuning-benefit narrative. It adds neighbor treatment fraction but not the proposed curvature features or tuning rule; it is not a same-feature tuning ablation.\\\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"results_focus\\\": [\n+    \\\"Lead with an absolute-error table for all four methods: equal-weight mean of the 24 family-by-condition RMSEs and mean absolute cell bias, with seed-block uncertainty computed from existing final records. The selected method's recorded mean capped normalized RMSE is 0.6140475146408374 and mean absolute capped normalized bias is 0.4597691498768297. Check clipping explicitly before equating these with uncapped quantities.\\\",\n+    \\\"Preserve the unfavorable aggregate baseline comparison in the main text. Descriptive reaggregation of final.evidence.episodes gives equal-cell mean uncapped RMSE 0.4598497552813962 for one-hop OLS and 1.36760394415568 for difference in means, versus 0.6140475146408373 for the selected estimator. These are deterministic summaries of existing observations, not newly run experiments, confidence intervals, or proof of statistical significance; the writer must materialize their derivation and grounding before reporting rounded values.\\\",\n+    \\\"Show the complete four-family by six-condition error matrix through matched RMSE ratios to each of the three baselines, backed by absolute errors in the table and supplement. Retain no-spillover regressions, threshold failures, and omitted two-hop failures as central evidence rather than highlighting only favorable strong-spillover comparisons.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use candidate observed_fit_rmse versus absolute uncapped GATE error to illustrate why in-sample fit is not an endpoint-accuracy certificate. Recorded fit residuals are not test prediction error, and no baseline observed-fit measurements or predictive-GCV comparison are available.\\\",\n+    \\\"Compare the strong linear spillover condition with its cluster-assignment counterpart within graph families using the same seed blocks. This is a bounded design comparison, not evidence that graph clustering is optimal or that support alone causes the error change. Treat minimum/maximum exposure and endpoint fractions as descriptive supporting measurements, not identification tests.\\\",\n+    \\\"Separate scientific performance from evaluation integrity. Anchor the reported final completion and qualification only in final.qualification.*; do not display the composite utility as GATE accuracy. Development results, unscored branches, and search counts do not enter main comparisons. No study.* sources exist to support reporting campaign selection statistics or search score differences.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"figure_plan\\\": [\n+    \\\"Figure 1, early on page 1 or 2: a conceptual overview of observed network experiment, one-hop exposure regression with contrast-targeted tuning, and the all-treated minus all-control prediction target, explicitly separating observed fitting from counterfactual endpoint prediction.\\\",\n+    \\\"Figure 2: four matched-comparison panels, one per graph family, showing the selected estimator's RMSE divided by each recorded baseline's RMSE across all six conditions; pair with a main-text absolute-error table so relative plots cannot conceal poor absolute performance.\\\",\n+    \\\"Figure 3: a two-panel diagnostic composite juxtaposing in-sample fit with endpoint error and the matched individual-versus-cluster assignment comparison for strong linear spillover. Show failures and uncertainty, not search trajectories.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"figure_specs\\\": [\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"endpoint_overview\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Make the global intervention target and the distinction between observed fitting and endpoint prediction legible at a glance, without asserting empirical calibration of the tuning rule.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"diagram\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"generate-concept-figure\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"observed inputs, fitted regression, endpoint contrast\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"none; conceptual relationships only\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"none\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 0.98,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"The inspected paper.sty is single-column, with 5.5in text width and 9in text height; column width equals full text width, not a 2.6in double-column slot. Target 5.39in width by about 1.7-1.9in height, with caption occupying about one quarter page in total. Place on page 1 or 2. Use one cohesive panel with at most three horizontal zones, at most six short conceptual labels, no tiny equations, and rendered text at least 8.5pt. All-treated/all-control are prediction inputs, not observed outcome labels. Use an editable draw.io source, not invented empirical curves; the task's graph sketch is explicitly schematic.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 1,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 1\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"conditional_accuracy\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Expose both improvements over assignment-only baselines and regressions relative to one-hop OLS in every final graph-by-condition cell, using final.evidence.episodes only.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"faceted_dot_whisker\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"baseline comparator; selected estimator is the common numerator\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"cell RMSE(selected) / cell RMSE(comparator), computed from uncapped final errors; below one favors the selected estimator\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"graph family, with six condition rows in each panel\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Use a 2-by-2 grid over the inspected 5.5in single text column, about 3.8-4.2in high plus a concise caption, roughly half a body page. Each approximately 2.6in-wide panel has six horizontal condition rows and three dodged comparator markers, a shared logarithmic ratio axis, and a vertical reference at one; use one shared three-entry legend. Labels such as No spill, Weak linear, Strong linear, Threshold, Two-hop, and Cluster are defined in the protocol table. Render labels at least 8.5pt, markers clearly separated, and at most two panels per row. Compute ratios of per-cell RMSEs, not means of per-seed ratios. Seed-block bootstrap intervals are planned summaries, not currently supplied estimates; do not attach significance stars. Report absolute errors beside this figure in a main-text table.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 4,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 3\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"diagnostic_limits\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Show that near-noise-level in-sample residuals can coexist with endpoint error, and summarize the bounded assignment-design contrast without causal attribution to support or tuning.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"scatter_and_paired_dot_whisker\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"panel A: threshold, two-hop, and remaining conditions; panel B: assignment design\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"panel A: observed-fit RMSE versus absolute uncapped GATE error; panel B: selected-estimator RMSE under strong linear spillover with individual versus cluster assignment\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"two diagnostic questions; panel B has four graph-family categories\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Use two panels in one row across the inspected 5.5in single-column text width, approximately 2.4-2.7in high plus caption, about one third page. Panel A uses transparent points and three semantic groups, not six colored mechanism series; show all scientific records and no fitted causal trend. Its observations are seed-dependent and are not independent sample-size evidence. Panel B has four graph categories and two design series with seed-block uncertainty, not four overlapping method curves. Keep labels at least 8.5pt and use the same condition names as Figure 2. If crowding occurs, stack the two panels and revise the page allocation rather than shrinking text; all support-fraction tables remain in the supplement.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 4,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 3\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"supplementary_plan\\\": {\n+    \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"After the references, provide the complete recorded conditional evidence, implementation specification, and reproducibility/audit boundaries needed to inspect the main thesis; do not use the supplement to hide the unfavorable one-hop OLS comparison or the principal threshold/two-hop failures.\\\",\n+    \\\"planned_subsections\\\": [\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Complete Conditional Error Tables\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Tabulate signed bias, absolute bias, uncapped RMSE, and replicate counts for all four methods in every graph-by-condition cell, using validated true_gate, contrast, and baseline_estimates. Preserve pairing and distinguish mean cell RMSE from pooled RMSE. Include uncertainty derived from the existing seed records and describe its resampling unit. These are reanalyses, not new simulation runs.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.mean_capped_normalized_rmse\\\", \\\"final.metric.mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Exposure Support and Residual Diagnostics\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Provide existing exposure minima/maxima, fractions at zero and one, linear-design condition measurements, and observed-fit residual summaries by family and condition. Relate them descriptively to endpoint errors and individual/cluster assignment. The condition measurement is for the evaluator's simple linear design, not the selected quadratic reference; none of these fields certifies endpoint identifiability. No new calibration or coverage experiment is implied.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Estimator Specification and Conditional Risk Criterion\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Document the available selected source at results/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1/src/program.py: feature map, saved centering/scaling, SVD rank tolerance, contrast row-space tolerance, residual degrees of freedom, the n times {0, 1e-6, 1e-4, 1e-2, 1} penalty grid, deterministic minimum selection, constant bypass, and fixed-hinge fallback. The result source links to the selected source directory; final source IDs identify its evaluated evidence, not an independently verified implementation hash. Distinguish a fixed-penalty risk identity under a correct reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian noise from the optimistic selected minimum. No selected-penalty distribution, fallback frequency, or empirical risk calibration is recorded.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"result.best_correct_score\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Benchmark Construction and Reproduction\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Use task/resources/final/manifest.json, task/task_contract.json, task/requirements.txt, and task/evaluate_final.py as local protocol provenance alongside the final records. Specify graph generation, all six outcome/design conditions, covariates, unit Gaussian noise, the deterministic BFS-cluster design, preserved isolates, and length-two walks with backtracking. Separate synthetic topology replication from repeated assignments/outcomes on the fixed Stanford3 graph. Record seeds, checksums, declared dependency versions, recorded runtime limits versus measured elapsed time, and exact reproduction commands only after checking available entrypoints. Map each eventual table/figure to a source script; those plot scripts do not exist yet. Explain capped metrics and equal-cell aggregation, and retain required graph attribution and share-alike caveats.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Evaluation Integrity and Missing Evidence\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Report completed final evaluation and its reported qualification from final.qualification.* separately from statistical accuracy, and summarize constant-outcome, relabeling, and replay checks only to their documented scope. Candidate internal diagnostics are marked unverified. State that original fixed-hinge development evidence is not a matched final comparator, component ablations are absent, and broader stress studies were not performed. A brief nonchronological missing-evidence note may cite the local two-hop branch's evaluation_failure.json and harness_evaluate_stderr.log: disconnection is observed, underlying cause and performance unknown. Do not report campaign counts, winner margins, or search deltas without study.* grounding; do not reinterpret a missing score as a loss.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\", \\\"final.qualification.correct\\\", \\\"final.metric.control_success_fraction\\\", \\\"final.metric.failed_episode_fraction\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"LLM Usage\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Include the venue-required separate section disclosing documented LLM roles in ideation, candidate-code generation, reference-query planning, framing, writing, and figure preparation as applicable. Use actual process artifacts under results/ideas and the local strategy/agent records; distinguish work already performed from future writing/figure activity. These process artifacts, not scientific metric source IDs, ground the disclosure. Include no author identities, personal links, funding, or acknowledgements.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": []\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  },\n+  \\\"forbidden_headings\\\": [\n+    \\\"Search Progression\\\",\n+    \\\"Campaign Phases\\\",\n+    \\\"Milestones\\\",\n+    \\\"Timeline of Improvements\\\",\n+    \\\"First Successful Estimator\\\",\n+    \\\"Experiment A / Experiment B\\\",\n+    \\\"From Baseline to Winner\\\",\n+    \\\"Architecture Sweep\\\",\n+    \\\"Ablation Results (when no component ablations exist)\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"forbidden_phrases\\\": [\n+    \\\"we then tried\\\",\n+    \\\"first valid result\\\",\n+    \\\"subsequent improvements\\\",\n+    \\\"next-best\\\",\n+    \\\"later variants\\\",\n+    \\\"search winner proves superiority\\\",\n+    \\\"universally outperforms\\\",\n+    \\\"state of the art\\\",\n+    \\\"robust to arbitrary interference\\\",\n+    \\\"validated endpoint-risk calibration\\\",\n+    \\\"unbiased after tuning\\\",\n+    \\\"predictive GCV is inferior\\\",\n+    \\\"the two-hop method failed scientifically\\\",\n+    \\\"eligible\\\",\n+    \\\"correct and eligible\\\",\n+    \\\"correctness rate\\\",\n+    \\\"combined_score\\\",\n+    \\\"worker_01\\\",\n+    \\\"worker_02\\\",\n+    \\\"rubric\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"notes_for_writer\\\": [\n+    \\\"Follow write-paper-standalone only at the later drafting stage. This strategy is ready for a bounded comparison paper, not a claim of publication acceptance, novel causal identification, or final-deliverable completeness. Do not draft paper.tex, claims_grounding.json, or run final paper validation during strategy generation.\\\",\n+    \\\"ICLR 2026 is fixed by VENUE.md: anonymized conference submission, 8-9 body pages excluding references; an eight-page body must finish at least 75% full. No names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, self-identifying repositories, or internal URLs. Include a separate LLM Usage section, preferably after references. The supplied paper.sty confirms single-column 5.5in by 9in geometry; its historical header does not independently verify official 2026 template provenance. Check official-template provenance during final preparation and regenerate figures if geometry differs, rather than silently treating this as a double-column format.\\\",\n+    \\\"Target approximately 8.75 substantive body pages including figures and tables: title/abstract 0.40; introduction and early overview 1.10; related work 0.65; target and interference setup 0.90; endpoint-tuned estimator 1.50; empirical protocol and condition table 0.95; conditional comparisons and diagnostic figures 2.15; discussion/limitations 0.85; conclusion 0.25. These are layout allocations, not measured page counts. Compress repetition rather than exceeding nine pages; spend spare space explaining the risk formula, baseline definitions, aggregation, uncertainty, and failure cases, never diary or filler.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use thesis-first headings such as Global Intervention Target, Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression, Empirical Protocol, Conditional Accuracy and Failure, and Scope and Limitations. Present all networks as condition-specific evidence under one question. The introduction's contributions are an implemented target-aware regression construction, a matched conditional comparison, and an explicit accuracy/fit limitation map; do not claim isolated benefits of curvature features or risk tuning.\\\",\n+    \\\"Define GATE as the finite-population average difference between all-treated and all-control potential outcomes. The fitted estimate averages node-aligned predictions at those two assignments. Define neighbor exposure with isolates assigned zero; all-treated exposure need not equal one for isolates. Describe two-hop interference as length-two random-walk exposure including backtracking, not unique-distance-two exposure or the failed branch's nonbacktracking features.\\\",\n+    \\\"Map condition IDs to scientific names using the final manifest: no spillover; weak linear neighbor spillover; strong linear neighbor spillover; threshold-plus-treatment interaction; two-hop spillover; and strong linear spillover under deterministic graph-BFS cluster assignment. The last condition changes assignment, not the outcome mechanism relative to strong linear spillover. All outcomes are synthetic, including on Stanford3; no real platform outcome or social causal effect is measured.\\\",\n+    \\\"Explain the implemented endpoint-risk score as (a_lambda\"'^T y_centered - a_0''^T y_centered)''^2 + 2*sigma_hat''^2*a_lambda''^T*a_0 - sigma_hat''^2*||a_0||''^2, where a_0 represents the estimable reference contrast and a_lambda the fixed-penalty contrast. State the correct-reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian-noise assumptions, the row-space and degrees-of-freedom guards, and the lack of an unbiased post-selection interpretation. These are implementation/conditional analytical facts, not evidence that the assumptions hold in every condition or a new established theorem.\",\n+    \"No same-feature fixed-penalty, OLS, or predictive-GCV ablations have usable final evidence. The proposed estimator jointly changes feature representation and regularization relative to the original fixed-hinge regression; comparisons with simple one-hop OLS likewise change more than one component. Do not attribute any improvement or regression solely to tuning, curvature, support, conditioning, or the fallback. Do not turn algebraic estimability into positivity, identification, or generalization.\",\n+    \"Uncertainty must be recomputed from the 24 existing seed-level episode records, preserving every graph/condition/method observation within a resampled seed block. For ratios, recompute both methods'\"' RMSEs in each resample before dividing; for the table, resample blocks before equal-cell averaging. A planned 95% paired seed-block percentile bootstrap with a fixed documented random seed may be used; specify resample count when implemented. No intervals have yet been produced. These quantify Monte Carlo variation in this fixed benchmark, not unit-level causal confidence-interval coverage or independent graph-population generalization. Per-condition comparisons are descriptive/exploratory and multiplicity is not resolved by the bootstrap.\\\",\n+    \\\"Ground every scientific result view in final.evidence.episodes and the appropriate final.metric.* fields. The final qualification facts are completed, qualified, and true under final.qualification.status/outcome/correct, independently of downstream study metadata. Gate diagnostics not provided remain unknown; infer no validity/adaptivity failure or guarantee. If authoritative later evidence reports not_qualified, retain that outcome and its stated limitations in writing and review; do not infer its cause from retained metrics or erase it on publication grounds.\\\",\n+    \\\"PRIVATE-ONLY provenance: paper_data.best_correct, result.best_correct_score, and results/best_program_manifest.json locate the available selected source; the final manifest supplies the frozen program-tree identifier. downstream_study and study.* are absent. Do not invent study.* IDs, expose selection-score internals in visible prose, report scored/unscored attempt counts, or subtract search and final scores. Do not silently treat the development-only original fixed-hinge method as a recorded final baseline. Missing two-hop performance does not justify eliminating two-hop adjustment as a scientific possibility.\\\",\n+    \\\"Keep copy-observed and constant-prediction checks out of the substantive four-method comparison; they are degenerate behavioral controls, not credible scientific alternatives. Report task integrity checks briefly and do not let a high composite utility stand in for effect-estimation accuracy. Do not invent inference coverage, runtimes per estimator, selected-penalty histograms, contrast-weight norms, support probabilities, or fallback rates from fields that do not exist.\\\",\n+    \\\"Required figure budgets are explicit: overview one panel; accuracy four panels arranged two per row; diagnostics two panels in one row. Never exceed three panels per row, six categories per panel, or four series per panel; the tighter figure-specific limits govern. All figures target the single full text column. Pair the quantitative comparison with a compact main table of absolute RMSE and absolute bias for all four methods, and a protocol table defining conditions and graph sizes. Do not substitute a favorable-cell ranking for the complete condition matrix.\\\",\n+    \\\"Vector-first output: quantitative plotting scripts must export both PDF and 300-DPI PNG from the same source; LaTeX must use the PDF whenever it exists. The overview uses generate-concept-figure exactly once and preserves editable draw.io source; request a vector PDF export if available, otherwise regenerate raster output at least 2400px wide for this full-width placement. Any column-width raster in a changed layout must be at least 1200px wide. Never upscale existing rasters. If the concept workflow is impossible or fails, log the concrete reason before any documented vector fallback; do not silently replace it with an improvised schematic.\\\",\n+    \\\"References are not an experiment. references.bib currently contains permitted starter-title matches Chin2018RegressionAF and Han2023ModelbasedRA, but references_provenance.json records retrieval metadata, not verified reading, and other returned statistical titles are not automatically relevant foundations for this criterion. Cite only existing keys after checking and reading permitted sources. Do not pad related work with unrelated retrieved hits, rely on questionable metadata years without checking, or invent missing graph-attribution citation keys. Any additional acquisition belongs to an authorized reference step, not to claims grounded in this strategy.\\\",\n+    \\\"Respect the restricted-literature boundary: do not identify the prohibited paper, expand citation graphs, or read related-paper/reproduction/discussion pages. Allowed starter-source checks and graph provenance must not become unrestricted searches. If restricted material is encountered, stop and record where and how much was seen. No external research or new scientific runs were performed during this framing stage.\\\",\n+    \\\"The supplement is a minimum grounded scope, not an authoritative posthoc ranking. Route later appendix-ranked analyses there without retroactively changing this strategy, and keep evidence essential to the central thesis in the main paper. Proposed missing studies, such as matched tuning ablations, noise stress tests, hidden endpoint twins, and inference calibration, belong in limitations/future work and must never be written as completed results.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n+}\n*** Add File: /workspace/paper/paper_strategy.md\n+# Paper Strategy\n+\n+**Status:** ready for a bounded standalone comparison study, not a declaration of publication readiness.  \n+**Prompt family:** \"'`paper_standalone`; downstream skill: `write-paper-standalone`.  \n+**Provisional title:** Endpoint-Tuned Regression under Network Interference: Accuracy and Limits.\n+\n+## Central Question and Thesis\n+\n+**Question.** How accurately does endpoint-risk-tuned one-hop regression estimate the global average treatment effect across the evaluated networks, interference mechanisms, and assignment designs, relative to simple regression baselines?\n+\n+**Thesis.** In the evaluated synthetic-outcome settings, endpoint-tuned exposure regression reduces error relative to assignment-only comparators in many spillover conditions but performs worse in aggregate than linear one-hop OLS, while near-noise-level in-sample residuals coexist with substantial endpoint errors under threshold and omitted two-hop interference.\n+\n+This is one shared thesis with condition-specific evidence across SBM, BA, WS, and Stanford3, not four separate experiment narratives. The contribution is an implemented target-aware regression construction and an honest map of its conditional accuracy and limits. It is not a new identification theorem, proof that target-aware tuning improves estimation, or demonstration of general superiority.\n+\n+## Why This Story Wins\n+\n+The completed final evaluation provides matched estimates for the frozen method and three substantive baselines. `final.evidence.episodes` contains 24 seed blocks, covering 576 scientific cases across four graph families and six conditions. This evidence supports a direct performance-and-limitations paper even though the stronger exposure-aware baseline is better in aggregate.\n+\n+Descriptive reaggregation performed during framing gives equal-cell mean uncapped RMSE of approximately 0.6140 for the selected estimator, 0.4598 for one-hop OLS, and 1.3676 for difference in means. The selected estimator'\"'s authoritative recorded mean capped normalized RMSE is 0.6140475146408374; its mean absolute capped normalized bias is 0.4597691498768297. The reaggregated baseline values come from existing final observations, not new simulations or uncertainty estimates. The writer must save the derivation and ground the resulting table before publishing rounded values, and verify clipping before equating raw and capped summaries.\n+\n+The following alternatives are rejected:\n+\n+- **Positive development-improvement story:** a development score does not establish final superiority, and the final one-hop OLS comparison contradicts a general-benefit claim.\n+- **Broad method/architecture family:** no usable final comparison supports a neural or two-hop estimator family, and component ablations are absent.\n+- **Support or tuning as a proven causal explanation:** measured support and fit are descriptive; feature changes and tuning are not isolated.\n+- **Search chronology:** does not answer the scientific accuracy question and would conceal the strongest comparisons behind development events.\n+\n+Negative and conditional findings are the reason to write this paper, not a reason to omit the method or manufacture an improvement.\n+\n+## Framing and Input Audit\n+\n+**Decision: recentered.** This is not an edit-mode manuscript revision. No resolved edit-context input, prior manuscript, \"'`aggregated/`, `extra_tasks/`, or contributions scaffold was supplied. The label denotes recentering the source task'\"'s broad research agenda on the narrower final evidence, not revising an imaginary prior paper. Preservation is therefore inapplicable; broadening lacks evidence; an evidence-led comparison is the defensible choice.\n+\n+The inspected selected source is \"'`results/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1/src/program.py`, located through `paper_data.best_correct` and `results/best_program_manifest.json`. It implements one-hop quadratic exposure regression and endpoint-risk ridge tuning, not a two-hop model. The original source at `task/program_0/src/program.py` uses fixed hinges and fixed ridge. The final manifest identifies the frozen program tree, but an independent file-by-file identity check is not claimed here.\n+\n+`paper_data.json.final_evaluation` was read independently of study metadata. The grounded values are:\n+\n+- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n+- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n+- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n+\n+These are reported task-specific completion/integrity facts, not evidence of superiority, generalization, model validity in every setting, or publication acceptance. Missing gate diagnostics remain unknown. If later authoritative evidence reports failed qualification, retain that outcome and its stated limitations through drafting, review, and revision; do not infer its cause from performance metrics or reinterpret publication eligibility as scientific qualification.\n+\n+No `downstream_study` or `study.*` sources exist. Do not invent them or report campaign winner margins, search-score deltas, or scored/unscored attempt counts. **Private-only provenance:** development pointers locate the selected implementation; they are not main-paper performance evidence.\n+\n+The original fixed-hinge estimator lacks a matched final comparison with the frozen method. Omit that final contrast rather than comparing development and final scores. A failed two-hop GCV branch has no usable performance evidence: its local stderr reports disconnection before candidate responses, while the underlying cause remains unknown. Mention missing evidence nonchronologically if needed; never call it an estimator loss or an invalid scientific method.\n+\n+## Methods to Include\n+\n+| Method group | Role | Scientific purpose |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| Endpoint-risk-tuned quadratic one-hop regression | Proposed | The frozen evaluated construction and its guarded endpoint objective |\n+| Difference in means | Baseline | What a direct assignment contrast misses under interference; retain its favorable no-spillover evidence |\n+| Own-treatment OLS with covariates | Baseline | Ordinary adjustment without explicit exposure representation |\n+| Linear one-hop OLS with covariates | Baseline | Essential stronger comparison; it is better in aggregate and is not a same-feature tuning ablation |\n+\n+Do not expand the main comparison with copy-observed or constant-prediction behavioral checks. The original fixed-hinge method and incomplete two-hop branch belong only in implementation provenance/missing-evidence discussion, without an unsupported final comparison.\n+\n+### Explanation Worth the Body Space\n+\n+Define finite-population GATE as the average all-treated versus all-control potential-outcome difference. The estimator averages the corresponding fitted node predictions. Neighbor exposure is zero for isolates, so all-treated exposure is not automatically one for every unit.\n+\n+Explain the actual feature map: intercept, covariates, own treatment, neighbor treatment fraction, treatment-exposure interaction, exposure curvature, and treatment-curvature interaction. Describe saved centering/scaling, SVD, the fixed penalty grid, contrast estimability and residual-degrees-of-freedom guards, fixed-hinge fallback, and constant-outcome bypass. Their implementation does not establish how frequently the guards or penalties were used.\n+\n+The implemented risk criterion for a fixed penalty is\n+\n+`(a_lambda''^T y_centered - a_0''^T y_centered)''^2 + 2 sigma_hat''^2 a_lambda''^T a_0 - sigma_hat''^2 ||a_0||''^2`,\n+\n+where `a_0` represents the estimable reference contrast. Explain the correct-reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian-noise assumptions. A fixed-penalty risk identity is not unbiased risk reporting after selecting its minimum. Algebraic estimability is not endpoint support, identification, or robustness. Do not promote an unverified derivation into a new theorem.\n+\n+The candidate changes both representation and tuning relative to the original fixed-hinge regression. No same-feature predictive-GCV, fixed-penalty, or unregularized ablation has usable final evidence. The paper cannot assign gains or regressions to any single component.\n+\n+## Results That Carry the Thesis\n+\n+1. **Absolute accuracy first.** A compact main-text table reports all four methods'\"' equal-cell mean RMSE and mean absolute cell bias, with uncertainty derived from existing records. Do not substitute pooled RMSE, mean absolute error, or a composite utility for these definitions. Make the unfavorable aggregate one-hop OLS comparison explicit.\n+2. **Complete conditional comparison.** Show every final family-by-condition cell, including no spillover, weak and strong linear spillover, threshold interaction, omitted two-hop spillover, and strong linear spillover under cluster assignment. Preserve regressions and large failures in the main figures.\n+3. **Fit is not endpoint accuracy.** Plot the selected estimator's observed-fit residual RMSE against absolute GATE error. Call it in-sample residual error, not held-out prediction performance; no baseline residual measurements or predictive-GCV comparison are available.\n+4. **Bounded assignment-design contrast.** Compare strong linear spillover under individual assignment with its graph-BFS cluster counterpart within families and seed blocks. Do not infer optimal clustering or causally attribute the change to support alone.\n+5. **Supporting diagnostics.** Summarize recorded exposure ranges, endpoint fractions, and the evaluator's simple-linear-design condition measurement. The condition measurement is not that of the selected quadratic reference. Candidate internal diagnostics are unverified; penalty frequencies, fallback frequencies, risk calibration, and inference coverage are absent.\n+\n+The threshold condition combines a neighbor-exposure threshold with a treatment interaction. The two-hop condition uses length-two walks **including backtracking**, not a unique-distance-two average. The cluster condition changes assignment relative to strong linear spillover, not the outcome formula. Use the actual manifest definitions rather than a generic interference taxonomy. All outcomes, including Stanford3 outcomes, are synthetic.\n+\n+### Uncertainty and Aggregation\n+\n+Preserve the complete seed block across graph families, conditions, and methods when resampling the 24 final seed records. A planned 95% paired seed-block percentile bootstrap can summarize Monte Carlo uncertainty; document the random seed and resample count when implemented. For each resample, recompute per-cell RMSEs before forming ratios and recompute equal-cell averages before forming table intervals. No confidence intervals have yet been generated.\n+\n+The 576 cases are not 576 independent inferential replicates. The resampling quantifies Monte Carlo variation in this benchmark, not unit-level causal interval coverage. Stanford3 is one fixed public topology with repeated generated experiments, not a sample of 24 public networks. Synthetic graph variation and repeated observations on a fixed public graph have different interpretations. Avoid significance stars and posthoc win counts; per-condition comparisons are exploratory and bootstrap intervals do not resolve multiplicity.\n+\n+## Figure and Table Contract\n+\n+### Template Geometry\n+\n+\"'`VENUE.md` fixes ICLR 2026 and an 8-9-page body excluding references. The inspected `paper.sty` specifies a **single text column, 5.5 inches wide and 9 inches high**. Here column width equals full text width; there is no double-column half-width slot. Use full-width figures rather than shrinking dense panels into imagined narrow columns. The style'\"'s historical header is not independent proof of official 2026 release provenance; verify that during final preparation and regenerate assets if geometry changes.\n+\n+All figure labels must remain at least 8.5 pt at final placement. Maximum budgets are figure-specific and never exceed four panels total, three panels per row, six categories per panel, or four series per panel. Page-footprint estimates include captions and are planning allocations, not compiled measurements.\n+\n+| Figure | Content and generator | Geometry and readability | Placement / footprint |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| \"'`endpoint_overview` | Required conceptual diagram via `generate-concept-figure`; observed experiment → exposure regression and endpoint-risk tuning → mean all-treated minus all-control prediction | One cohesive panel, at most three horizontal zones, six short conceptual labels; 0.98 text width, about 5.39 × 1.7-1.9 in; no fabricated curves or numeric results | Page 1 or 2; about one quarter page |\n+| `conditional_accuracy` | Required matplotlib 2×2 dot-whisker comparison; each family has six condition rows and three ratios, selected RMSE divided by baseline RMSE | Full 5.5 in width, about 3.8-4.2 in high; two panels per row; six categories, three series per panel; shared log ratio axis and line at one; shared three-entry legend | Main results; roughly half page |\n+| `diagnostic_limits` | Required matplotlib composite: residual-fit/error scatter plus selected-estimator individual-versus-cluster RMSE | Full width, about 2.4-2.7 in high; two panels in one row. Scatter groups threshold, two-hop, and remaining conditions into three visual series; design panel has four families and two series | Main results; about one third page |\n+\n+For Figure 2, values below one favor the selected method; use ratios of cell RMSEs, never averages of per-run ratios. Intervals are planned reanalyses of recorded data. Keep a main-text table of absolute errors beside the relative comparison so ratios cannot hide poor absolute accuracy. Define the six short condition labels in a compact protocol table.\n+\n+For Figure 3, use transparent scatter points and no fitted causal trend. Correlated case records are visual observations, not extra independent replications. The assignment-design panel uses paired-seed uncertainty. Keep support-fraction tables in the supplement. If two panels cannot remain legible, stack them and adjust the body allocation rather than reduce type size.\n+\n+The overview is a semantic schematic, not an empirical network sample. Endpoint assignments are prediction inputs; endpoint outcomes are withheld from fitting. Use `generate-concept-figure` for exactly this one diagram and preserve its editable draw.io source. This stage only plans figures and does not invoke generation.\n+\n+### Export Requirements\n+\n+- Quantitative source scripts export both PDF and PNG, with the PNG at 300 DPI. LaTeX references the PDF whenever available.\n+- For the concept figure, request a vector PDF export from editable source if supported. If raster-only, regenerate at least 2400 px wide for full-width placement.\n+- Any raster assigned to a genuinely narrow column in a later changed layout needs at least 1200 px width. Never upscale existing raster output.\n+- If the concept workflow is impossible or fails, record the concrete failure before a documented vector fallback. Do not silently replace the required workflow with an improvised drawing.\n+- Plot scripts and figure assets do not yet exist; do not describe them as completed deliverables.\n+\n+## Body-Page Budget\n+\n+Target approximately **8.75 substantive pages**, including figures and tables:\n+\n+| Material | Page allocation |\n+| --- | ---: |\n+| Title and abstract | 0.40 |\n+| Introduction and early overview | 1.10 |\n+| Related work | 0.65 |\n+| Target and interference setup | 0.90 |\n+| Endpoint-tuned estimator | 1.50 |\n+| Empirical protocol and condition table | 0.95 |\n+| Conditional comparisons and diagnostic figures | 2.15 |\n+| Discussion and limitations | 0.85 |\n+| Conclusion | 0.25 |\n+\n+Use spare space for the endpoint objective, baseline definitions, conditional-risk assumptions, uncertainty/aggregation, and concrete failures. Do not add methods or studies to fill pages. Keep the main body within nine pages; an eight-page version must have its last page at least 75% full. Figures'\"' footprints are included in the relevant section allocations, not extra pages.\n+\n+Suggested thesis-first headings are **Global Intervention Target**, **Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression**, **Empirical Protocol**, **Conditional Accuracy and Failure**, and **Scope and Limitations**. The abstract and conclusion must retain the negative aggregate comparison and the lack of isolated tuning evidence.\n+\n+## Supplementary Information After References\n+\n+The supplement adds grounded depth without hiding evidence essential to the thesis. It is a minimum plan, not an authoritative ranking of later posthoc analyses.\n+\n+1. **Complete Conditional Error Tables.** All methods and family-by-condition cells: signed bias, absolute bias, uncapped RMSE, replicate counts, and paired-seed uncertainty. Explain equal-cell versus pooled summaries. Sources: \"'`final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.mean_capped_normalized_rmse`, `final.metric.mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias`.\n+2. **Exposure Support and Residual Diagnostics.** Existing minima/maxima, endpoint fractions, simple-linear-design condition measurements, and in-sample residuals. Interpret descriptively; no identifiability, calibration, or coverage claim. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`.\n+3. **Estimator Specification and Conditional Risk Criterion.** Document the inspected selected implementation, tolerances, centered/scaled SVD, penalty grid `n × {0, 1e-6, 1e-4, 1e-2, 1}`, tie handling, fallback, and constant bypass. Contrast fixed-penalty reasoning with selected-minimum optimism. `result.best_correct_score` links to the selected source directory; `final.evidence.episodes` supplies evaluated results, not independent source-hash verification. Do not invent tuning or fallback distributions.\n+4. **Benchmark Construction and Reproduction.** Use `task/resources/final/manifest.json`, `task/task_contract.json`, `task/requirements.txt`, and `task/evaluate_final.py` alongside `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.status/outcome`. Specify graph and outcome generation, the actual BFS cluster rule, backtracking two-hop exposure, noise/covariates, isolates, seeds, checksums, declared dependencies, and commands verified against existing entrypoints. Separate runtime limits from measured elapsed time. Map eventual tables and figures to scripts only after those scripts exist. Preserve Stanford3 attribution and share-alike obligations.\n+5. **Evaluation Integrity and Missing Evidence.** Use `final.qualification.*`, the recorded control/failure metrics, and final episode evidence. Explain constant-outcome, relabeling, and replay checks only to their documented scope; internal diagnostics remain unverified. A nonchronological missing-evidence note may cite the two-hop branch'\"'s local failure JSON and stderr without converting an unscored attempt into a scientific loss. No unsupported search counts, margins, or matched original-method final results.\n+6. **LLM Usage.** A separate venue-required section disclosing the precise documented roles of LLMs in ideation, code generation, query planning, framing, and eventual writing/figure work. Use actual process artifacts under \"'`results/ideas/` and local planning records, not performance source IDs or assumptions. Disclose performed activity separately from work still planned, without identifying authors.\n+\n+No ablation study, noise stress test, hidden-endpoint twin simulation, risk-calibration study, or inference-coverage experiment is available. Such work may be named as missing or future work, never as supplementary results already obtained. Route later appendix-ranked analyses here without retroactively rewriting this strategy; keep essential negative findings in the main body.\n+\n+## Citation, Access, and Venue Guardrails\n+\n+`references.bib` includes relevant starter-title matches `Chin2018RegressionAF` and `Han2023ModelbasedRA`, but the supplied reference provenance records retrieval metadata rather than verified reading. Other retrieved statistical titles are not automatically appropriate foundations for the implemented risk criterion. Use only existing keys after checking and reading permitted sources; check questionable year metadata. Do not invent graph-attribution keys or pad the related work with irrelevant hits. Missing literature acquisition belongs to an authorized reference step.\n+\n+Do not identify or search for the prohibited paper, expand citation graphs, or read prohibited summaries, reproductions, or discussions. If accidental exposure occurs, stop and record the location and extent. This strategy stage performed no external research and no new scientific runs.\n+\n+The ICLR 2026 manuscript must be fully anonymous: no names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, self-identifying links, or internal URLs. Include the separate `LLM Usage` section after references if appropriate. Current readiness applies to framing only, not verified official-template provenance, citation completeness, completed plot assets, uncertainty computations, or compiled-page compliance.\n+\n+## Language That Would Break the Story\n+\n+**Forbidden headings:** Search Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; Timeline of Improvements; First Successful Estimator; Experiment A / Experiment B; From Baseline to Winner; Architecture Sweep; and an Ablation Results heading without actual component ablations.\n+\n+**Forbidden visible phrases:** “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “next-best,” “later variants,” “search winner proves superiority,” “universally outperforms,” “state of the art,” “robust to arbitrary interference,” “validated endpoint-risk calibration,” “unbiased after tuning,” “predictive GCV is inferior,” and “the two-hop method failed scientifically.”\n+\n+Keep internal labels such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `combined_score`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, and `rubric` out of visible manuscript prose. Do not use a favorable composite utility as GATE accuracy. No thresholded-rate plot is needed; if added later, define one reader-facing meaning per figure.\n+\n+## Handoff Boundary\n+\n+This stage writes only the mirrored strategy outputs and append-only memory updates. It does not draft `paper.tex`, create `claims_grounding.json`, generate figures, execute a new simulation, or run final-paper validation. Later writing must save derived summaries, compute uncertainty, inspect allowed sources, and preserve all stated limitations before claiming a complete paper.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\n)\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'apply_patch() { /usr/local/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/node_modules/@openai/codex-linux-x64/vendor/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/bin/codex --codex-run-as-apply-patch \"$@\"; }\napply_patch \"$(cat <<'\"'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /workspace/paper/paper_strategy.json\n+{\n+  \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\",\n+  \\\"selected_prompt_family\\\": \\\"paper_standalone\\\",\n+  \\\"recommended_title\\\": \\\"Endpoint-Tuned Regression under Network Interference: Accuracy and Limits\\\",\n+  \\\"central_question\\\": \\\"How accurately does endpoint-risk-tuned one-hop regression estimate the global average treatment effect across the evaluated networks, interference mechanisms, and assignment designs, relative to simple regression baselines?\\\",\n+  \\\"central_thesis\\\": \\\"In the evaluated synthetic-outcome settings, endpoint-tuned exposure regression reduces error relative to assignment-only comparators in many spillover conditions but performs worse in aggregate than linear one-hop OLS, while near-noise-level in-sample residuals coexist with substantial endpoint errors under threshold and omitted two-hop interference.\\\",\n+  \\\"narrative_rationale\\\": \\\"The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation, not the development score: final.evidence.episodes contains 24 seed blocks, each with four graph families and six scientific conditions, permitting direct matched comparisons of the frozen estimator with recorded baselines. The scientifically useful result is conditional accuracy and failure, including an unfavorable aggregate comparison with one-hop OLS. The four graph families belong to one shared question, with family-specific evidence rather than separate chronological experiments. Endpoint-targeted tuning is a concrete implemented method, but neither its isolated benefit nor the cause of every error is established. A universal-improvement story, a broad architecture comparison, and a search-progression story are weaker because component ablations and valid final results for those alternative families are absent.\\\",\n+  \\\"prior_paper_story_status\\\": \\\"recentered\\\",\n+  \\\"edit_framing_decision\\\": \\\"This is a fresh framing, not a manuscript-edit run: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper, aggregated, extra_tasks, or contributions scaffold is present. Recentered denotes moving from the source task's broad accuracy agenda to the supported endpoint-regression comparison and its limitations; it does not assert that a prior manuscript was revised. Preserving a hypothetical prior story would invent evidence. Broadening to a shared estimator/architecture family is unsupported. Centering the completed final comparison is preferable to either broadening or a development-improvement narrative.\\\",\n+  \\\"staged_input_impact\\\": [\n+    \\\"The inspected final-evaluated method's available source is results/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1/src/program.py, linked by paper_data.best_correct and results/best_program_manifest.json. It implements quadratic one-hop exposure features, endpoint-risk ridge selection, estimability checks, and fixed-hinge fallback; it is not a neural or implemented two-hop estimator. The final manifest records a frozen program-tree hash, but this strategy does not claim an independent file-by-file hash verification.\\\",\n+    \\\"The original implementation at task/program_0/src/program.py uses fixed one-hop hinges and fixed ridge regularization. Its development evidence is not a matched final comparison with the frozen estimator. Omit an original-versus-selected final contrast rather than mixing search and final results; the directly recorded final one-hop OLS comparator remains essential.\\\",\n+    \\\"paper_data.final_evaluation was read independently: final.qualification.status is completed, final.qualification.outcome is qualified, and final.qualification.correct is true. These reported checks do not establish superiority, model adequacy, generalization, or publication readiness. No downstream_study or study.* grounding sources exist in this bundle.\\\",\n+    \\\"The final records, including Stanford3 and the unfavorable comparisons, change the center of gravity from optimizing a development score to mapping conditional endpoint error. The empty figures directory and absent ablation_studies require new visualizations of existing records, not invented experiments.\\\",\n+    \\\"The two-hop GCV branch has a failed evaluation artifact and no usable performance result. Its stderr reports a disconnection before receiving candidate responses; the underlying cause and estimator performance remain unknown. It is missing evidence, not a measured loss or evidence against two-hop adjustment. No externally staged new/old task families or prior manuscript were supplied.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"method_groups\\\": [\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Endpoint-risk-tuned quadratic one-hop regression\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"proposed\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The frozen final-evaluated method: covariates, own treatment, neighbor treatment fraction, treatment-exposure interaction, exposure curvature, and treatment-curvature interaction; centered/scaled SVD ridge with a fixed penalty grid and endpoint-contrast risk criterion. Explain guards, constant-outcome bypass, and fixed-hinge fallback without claiming their observed frequencies.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Difference in means\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"A recorded final comparator that ignores interference and provides an interpretable reference for direct-treatment contrasts versus the global intervention target. Retain its favorable no-spillover results.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Own-treatment OLS with covariates\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"A recorded final comparator separating ordinary covariate adjustment from explicit exposure adjustment. Its similarity to difference in means is informative rather than grounds for silently dropping it.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Linear one-hop OLS with covariates\\\",\n+      \\\"role\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\n+      \\\"include_reason\\\": \\\"The strongest recorded aggregate final comparator and the central negative control against a universal tuning-benefit narrative. It adds neighbor treatment fraction but not the proposed curvature features or tuning rule; it is not a same-feature tuning ablation.\\\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"results_focus\\\": [\n+    \\\"Lead with an absolute-error table for all four methods: equal-weight mean of the 24 family-by-condition RMSEs and mean absolute cell bias, with seed-block uncertainty computed from existing final records. The selected method's recorded mean capped normalized RMSE is 0.6140475146408374 and mean absolute capped normalized bias is 0.4597691498768297. Check clipping explicitly before equating these with uncapped quantities.\\\",\n+    \\\"Preserve the unfavorable aggregate baseline comparison in the main text. Descriptive reaggregation of final.evidence.episodes gives equal-cell mean uncapped RMSE 0.4598497552813962 for one-hop OLS and 1.36760394415568 for difference in means, versus 0.6140475146408373 for the selected estimator. These are deterministic summaries of existing observations, not newly run experiments, confidence intervals, or proof of statistical significance; the writer must materialize their derivation and grounding before reporting rounded values.\\\",\n+    \\\"Show the complete four-family by six-condition error matrix through matched RMSE ratios to each of the three baselines, backed by absolute errors in the table and supplement. Retain no-spillover regressions, threshold failures, and omitted two-hop failures as central evidence rather than highlighting only favorable strong-spillover comparisons.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use candidate observed_fit_rmse versus absolute uncapped GATE error to illustrate why in-sample fit is not an endpoint-accuracy certificate. Recorded fit residuals are not test prediction error, and no baseline observed-fit measurements or predictive-GCV comparison are available.\\\",\n+    \\\"Compare the strong linear spillover condition with its cluster-assignment counterpart within graph families using the same seed blocks. This is a bounded design comparison, not evidence that graph clustering is optimal or that support alone causes the error change. Treat minimum/maximum exposure and endpoint fractions as descriptive supporting measurements, not identification tests.\\\",\n+    \\\"Separate scientific performance from evaluation integrity. Anchor the reported final completion and qualification only in final.qualification.*; do not display the composite utility as GATE accuracy. Development results, unscored branches, and search counts do not enter main comparisons. No study.* sources exist to support reporting campaign selection statistics or search score differences.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"figure_plan\\\": [\n+    \\\"Figure 1, early on page 1 or 2: a conceptual overview of observed network experiment, one-hop exposure regression with contrast-targeted tuning, and the all-treated minus all-control prediction target, explicitly separating observed fitting from counterfactual endpoint prediction.\\\",\n+    \\\"Figure 2: four matched-comparison panels, one per graph family, showing the selected estimator's RMSE divided by each recorded baseline's RMSE across all six conditions; pair with a main-text absolute-error table so relative plots cannot conceal poor absolute performance.\\\",\n+    \\\"Figure 3: a two-panel diagnostic composite juxtaposing in-sample fit with endpoint error and the matched individual-versus-cluster assignment comparison for strong linear spillover. Show failures and uncertainty, not search trajectories.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"figure_specs\\\": [\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"endpoint_overview\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Make the global intervention target and the distinction between observed fitting and endpoint prediction legible at a glance, without asserting empirical calibration of the tuning rule.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"diagram\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"generate-concept-figure\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"observed inputs, fitted regression, endpoint contrast\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"none; conceptual relationships only\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"none\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 0.98,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"The inspected paper.sty is single-column, with 5.5in text width and 9in text height; column width equals full text width, not a 2.6in double-column slot. Target 5.39in width by about 1.7-1.9in height, with caption occupying about one quarter page in total. Place on page 1 or 2. Use one cohesive panel with at most three horizontal zones, at most six short conceptual labels, no tiny equations, and rendered text at least 8.5pt. All-treated/all-control are prediction inputs, not observed outcome labels. Use an editable draw.io source, not invented empirical curves; the task's graph sketch is explicitly schematic.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 1,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 1\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"conditional_accuracy\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Expose both improvements over assignment-only baselines and regressions relative to one-hop OLS in every final graph-by-condition cell, using final.evidence.episodes only.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"faceted_dot_whisker\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"baseline comparator; selected estimator is the common numerator\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"cell RMSE(selected) / cell RMSE(comparator), computed from uncapped final errors; below one favors the selected estimator\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"graph family, with six condition rows in each panel\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Use a 2-by-2 grid over the inspected 5.5in single text column, about 3.8-4.2in high plus a concise caption, roughly half a body page. Each approximately 2.6in-wide panel has six horizontal condition rows and three dodged comparator markers, a shared logarithmic ratio axis, and a vertical reference at one; use one shared three-entry legend. Labels such as No spill, Weak linear, Strong linear, Threshold, Two-hop, and Cluster are defined in the protocol table. Render labels at least 8.5pt, markers clearly separated, and at most two panels per row. Compute ratios of per-cell RMSEs, not means of per-seed ratios. Seed-block bootstrap intervals are planned summaries, not currently supplied estimates; do not attach significance stars. Report absolute errors beside this figure in a main-text table.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 4,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 6,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 3\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"figure_id\\\": \\\"diagnostic_limits\\\",\n+      \\\"required\\\": true,\n+      \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Show that near-noise-level in-sample residuals can coexist with endpoint error, and summarize the bounded assignment-design contrast without causal attribution to support or tuning.\\\",\n+      \\\"figure_type\\\": \\\"scatter_and_paired_dot_whisker\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_generator\\\": \\\"matplotlib\\\",\n+      \\\"group_by\\\": \\\"panel A: threshold, two-hop, and remaining conditions; panel B: assignment design\\\",\n+      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"panel A: observed-fit RMSE versus absolute uncapped GATE error; panel B: selected-estimator RMSE under strong linear spillover with individual versus cluster assignment\\\",\n+      \\\"split_by\\\": \\\"two diagnostic questions; panel B has four graph-family categories\\\",\n+      \\\"preferred_span\\\": \\\"full_width\\\",\n+      \\\"target_width_fraction\\\": 1.0,\n+      \\\"layout_rationale\\\": \\\"Use two panels in one row across the inspected 5.5in single-column text width, approximately 2.4-2.7in high plus caption, about one third page. Panel A uses transparent points and three semantic groups, not six colored mechanism series; show all scientific records and no fitted causal trend. Its observations are seed-dependent and are not independent sample-size evidence. Panel B has four graph categories and two design series with seed-block uncertainty, not four overlapping method curves. Keep labels at least 8.5pt and use the same condition names as Figure 2. If crowding occurs, stack the two panels and revise the page allocation rather than shrinking text; all support-fraction tables remain in the supplement.\\\",\n+      \\\"allow_progression\\\": false,\n+      \\\"max_panels\\\": 2,\n+      \\\"max_categories_per_panel\\\": 4,\n+      \\\"max_series_per_panel\\\": 3\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"supplementary_plan\\\": {\n+    \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"After the references, provide the complete recorded conditional evidence, implementation specification, and reproducibility/audit boundaries needed to inspect the main thesis; do not use the supplement to hide the unfavorable one-hop OLS comparison or the principal threshold/two-hop failures.\\\",\n+    \\\"planned_subsections\\\": [\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Complete Conditional Error Tables\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Tabulate signed bias, absolute bias, uncapped RMSE, and replicate counts for all four methods in every graph-by-condition cell, using validated true_gate, contrast, and baseline_estimates. Preserve pairing and distinguish mean cell RMSE from pooled RMSE. Include uncertainty derived from the existing seed records and describe its resampling unit. These are reanalyses, not new simulation runs.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.metric.mean_capped_normalized_rmse\\\", \\\"final.metric.mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Exposure Support and Residual Diagnostics\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Provide existing exposure minima/maxima, fractions at zero and one, linear-design condition measurements, and observed-fit residual summaries by family and condition. Relate them descriptively to endpoint errors and individual/cluster assignment. The condition measurement is for the evaluator's simple linear design, not the selected quadratic reference; none of these fields certifies endpoint identifiability. No new calibration or coverage experiment is implied.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Estimator Specification and Conditional Risk Criterion\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Document the available selected source at results/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1/src/program.py: feature map, saved centering/scaling, SVD rank tolerance, contrast row-space tolerance, residual degrees of freedom, the n times {0, 1e-6, 1e-4, 1e-2, 1} penalty grid, deterministic minimum selection, constant bypass, and fixed-hinge fallback. The result source links to the selected source directory; final source IDs identify its evaluated evidence, not an independently verified implementation hash. Distinguish a fixed-penalty risk identity under a correct reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian noise from the optimistic selected minimum. No selected-penalty distribution, fallback frequency, or empirical risk calibration is recorded.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"result.best_correct_score\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Benchmark Construction and Reproduction\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Use task/resources/final/manifest.json, task/task_contract.json, task/requirements.txt, and task/evaluate_final.py as local protocol provenance alongside the final records. Specify graph generation, all six outcome/design conditions, covariates, unit Gaussian noise, the deterministic BFS-cluster design, preserved isolates, and length-two walks with backtracking. Separate synthetic topology replication from repeated assignments/outcomes on the fixed Stanford3 graph. Record seeds, checksums, declared dependency versions, recorded runtime limits versus measured elapsed time, and exact reproduction commands only after checking available entrypoints. Map each eventual table/figure to a source script; those plot scripts do not exist yet. Explain capped metrics and equal-cell aggregation, and retain required graph attribution and share-alike caveats.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\", \\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Evaluation Integrity and Missing Evidence\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Report completed final evaluation and its reported qualification from final.qualification.* separately from statistical accuracy, and summarize constant-outcome, relabeling, and replay checks only to their documented scope. Candidate internal diagnostics are marked unverified. State that original fixed-hinge development evidence is not a matched final comparator, component ablations are absent, and broader stress studies were not performed. A brief nonchronological missing-evidence note may cite the local two-hop branch's evaluation_failure.json and harness_evaluate_stderr.log: disconnection is observed, underlying cause and performance unknown. Do not report campaign counts, winner margins, or search deltas without study.* grounding; do not reinterpret a missing score as a loss.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"final.qualification.status\\\", \\\"final.qualification.outcome\\\", \\\"final.qualification.correct\\\", \\\"final.metric.control_success_fraction\\\", \\\"final.metric.failed_episode_fraction\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"]\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"LLM Usage\\\",\n+        \\\"grounded_content\\\": \\\"Include the venue-required separate section disclosing documented LLM roles in ideation, candidate-code generation, reference-query planning, framing, writing, and figure preparation as applicable. Use actual process artifacts under results/ideas and the local strategy/agent records; distinguish work already performed from future writing/figure activity. These process artifacts, not scientific metric source IDs, ground the disclosure. Include no author identities, personal links, funding, or acknowledgements.\\\",\n+        \\\"source_ids\\\": []\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  },\n+  \\\"forbidden_headings\\\": [\n+    \\\"Search Progression\\\",\n+    \\\"Campaign Phases\\\",\n+    \\\"Milestones\\\",\n+    \\\"Timeline of Improvements\\\",\n+    \\\"First Successful Estimator\\\",\n+    \\\"Experiment A / Experiment B\\\",\n+    \\\"From Baseline to Winner\\\",\n+    \\\"Architecture Sweep\\\",\n+    \\\"Ablation Results (when no component ablations exist)\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"forbidden_phrases\\\": [\n+    \\\"we then tried\\\",\n+    \\\"first valid result\\\",\n+    \\\"subsequent improvements\\\",\n+    \\\"next-best\\\",\n+    \\\"later variants\\\",\n+    \\\"search winner proves superiority\\\",\n+    \\\"universally outperforms\\\",\n+    \\\"state of the art\\\",\n+    \\\"robust to arbitrary interference\\\",\n+    \\\"validated endpoint-risk calibration\\\",\n+    \\\"unbiased after tuning\\\",\n+    \\\"predictive GCV is inferior\\\",\n+    \\\"the two-hop method failed scientifically\\\",\n+    \\\"eligible\\\",\n+    \\\"correct and eligible\\\",\n+    \\\"correctness rate\\\",\n+    \\\"combined_score\\\",\n+    \\\"worker_01\\\",\n+    \\\"worker_02\\\",\n+    \\\"rubric\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"notes_for_writer\\\": [\n+    \\\"Follow write-paper-standalone only at the later drafting stage. This strategy is ready for a bounded comparison paper, not a claim of publication acceptance, novel causal identification, or final-deliverable completeness. Do not draft paper.tex, claims_grounding.json, or run final paper validation during strategy generation.\\\",\n+    \\\"ICLR 2026 is fixed by VENUE.md: anonymized conference submission, 8-9 body pages excluding references; an eight-page body must finish at least 75% full. No names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, self-identifying repositories, or internal URLs. Include a separate LLM Usage section, preferably after references. The supplied paper.sty confirms single-column 5.5in by 9in geometry; its historical header does not independently verify official 2026 template provenance. Check official-template provenance during final preparation and regenerate figures if geometry differs, rather than silently treating this as a double-column format.\\\",\n+    \\\"Target approximately 8.75 substantive body pages including figures and tables: title/abstract 0.40; introduction and early overview 1.10; related work 0.65; target and interference setup 0.90; endpoint-tuned estimator 1.50; empirical protocol and condition table 0.95; conditional comparisons and diagnostic figures 2.15; discussion/limitations 0.85; conclusion 0.25. These are layout allocations, not measured page counts. Compress repetition rather than exceeding nine pages; spend spare space explaining the risk formula, baseline definitions, aggregation, uncertainty, and failure cases, never diary or filler.\\\",\n+    \\\"Use thesis-first headings such as Global Intervention Target, Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression, Empirical Protocol, Conditional Accuracy and Failure, and Scope and Limitations. Present all networks as condition-specific evidence under one question. The introduction's contributions are an implemented target-aware regression construction, a matched conditional comparison, and an explicit accuracy/fit limitation map; do not claim isolated benefits of curvature features or risk tuning.\\\",\n+    \\\"Define GATE as the finite-population average difference between all-treated and all-control potential outcomes. The fitted estimate averages node-aligned predictions at those two assignments. Define neighbor exposure with isolates assigned zero; all-treated exposure need not equal one for isolates. Describe two-hop interference as length-two random-walk exposure including backtracking, not unique-distance-two exposure or the failed branch's nonbacktracking features.\\\",\n+    \\\"Map condition IDs to scientific names using the final manifest: no spillover; weak linear neighbor spillover; strong linear neighbor spillover; threshold-plus-treatment interaction; two-hop spillover; and strong linear spillover under deterministic graph-BFS cluster assignment. The last condition changes assignment, not the outcome mechanism relative to strong linear spillover. All outcomes are synthetic, including on Stanford3; no real platform outcome or social causal effect is measured.\\\",\n+    \\\"Explain the implemented endpoint-risk score as (a_lambda\"'^T y_centered - a_0''^T y_centered)''^2 + 2*sigma_hat''^2*a_lambda''^T*a_0 - sigma_hat''^2*||a_0||''^2, where a_0 represents the estimable reference contrast and a_lambda the fixed-penalty contrast. State the correct-reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian-noise assumptions, the row-space and degrees-of-freedom guards, and the lack of an unbiased post-selection interpretation. These are implementation/conditional analytical facts, not evidence that the assumptions hold in every condition or a new established theorem.\",\n+    \"No same-feature fixed-penalty, OLS, or predictive-GCV ablations have usable final evidence. The proposed estimator jointly changes feature representation and regularization relative to the original fixed-hinge regression; comparisons with simple one-hop OLS likewise change more than one component. Do not attribute any improvement or regression solely to tuning, curvature, support, conditioning, or the fallback. Do not turn algebraic estimability into positivity, identification, or generalization.\",\n+    \"Uncertainty must be recomputed from the 24 existing seed-level episode records, preserving every graph/condition/method observation within a resampled seed block. For ratios, recompute both methods'\"' RMSEs in each resample before dividing; for the table, resample blocks before equal-cell averaging. A planned 95% paired seed-block percentile bootstrap with a fixed documented random seed may be used; specify resample count when implemented. No intervals have yet been produced. These quantify Monte Carlo variation in this fixed benchmark, not unit-level causal confidence-interval coverage or independent graph-population generalization. Per-condition comparisons are descriptive/exploratory and multiplicity is not resolved by the bootstrap.\\\",\n+    \\\"Ground every scientific result view in final.evidence.episodes and the appropriate final.metric.* fields. The final qualification facts are completed, qualified, and true under final.qualification.status/outcome/correct, independently of downstream study metadata. Gate diagnostics not provided remain unknown; infer no validity/adaptivity failure or guarantee. If authoritative later evidence reports not_qualified, retain that outcome and its stated limitations in writing and review; do not infer its cause from retained metrics or erase it on publication grounds.\\\",\n+    \\\"PRIVATE-ONLY provenance: paper_data.best_correct, result.best_correct_score, and results/best_program_manifest.json locate the available selected source; the final manifest supplies the frozen program-tree identifier. downstream_study and study.* are absent. Do not invent study.* IDs, expose selection-score internals in visible prose, report scored/unscored attempt counts, or subtract search and final scores. Do not silently treat the development-only original fixed-hinge method as a recorded final baseline. Missing two-hop performance does not justify eliminating two-hop adjustment as a scientific possibility.\\\",\n+    \\\"Keep copy-observed and constant-prediction checks out of the substantive four-method comparison; they are degenerate behavioral controls, not credible scientific alternatives. Report task integrity checks briefly and do not let a high composite utility stand in for effect-estimation accuracy. Do not invent inference coverage, runtimes per estimator, selected-penalty histograms, contrast-weight norms, support probabilities, or fallback rates from fields that do not exist.\\\",\n+    \\\"Required figure budgets are explicit: overview one panel; accuracy four panels arranged two per row; diagnostics two panels in one row. Never exceed three panels per row, six categories per panel, or four series per panel; the tighter figure-specific limits govern. All figures target the single full text column. Pair the quantitative comparison with a compact main table of absolute RMSE and absolute bias for all four methods, and a protocol table defining conditions and graph sizes. Do not substitute a favorable-cell ranking for the complete condition matrix.\\\",\n+    \\\"Vector-first output: quantitative plotting scripts must export both PDF and 300-DPI PNG from the same source; LaTeX must use the PDF whenever it exists. The overview uses generate-concept-figure exactly once and preserves editable draw.io source; request a vector PDF export if available, otherwise regenerate raster output at least 2400px wide for this full-width placement. Any column-width raster in a changed layout must be at least 1200px wide. Never upscale existing rasters. If the concept workflow is impossible or fails, log the concrete reason before any documented vector fallback; do not silently replace it with an improvised schematic.\\\",\n+    \\\"References are not an experiment. references.bib currently contains permitted starter-title matches Chin2018RegressionAF and Han2023ModelbasedRA, but references_provenance.json records retrieval metadata, not verified reading, and other returned statistical titles are not automatically relevant foundations for this criterion. Cite only existing keys after checking and reading permitted sources. Do not pad related work with unrelated retrieved hits, rely on questionable metadata years without checking, or invent missing graph-attribution citation keys. Any additional acquisition belongs to an authorized reference step, not to claims grounded in this strategy.\\\",\n+    \\\"Respect the restricted-literature boundary: do not identify the prohibited paper, expand citation graphs, or read related-paper/reproduction/discussion pages. Allowed starter-source checks and graph provenance must not become unrestricted searches. If restricted material is encountered, stop and record where and how much was seen. No external research or new scientific runs were performed during this framing stage.\\\",\n+    \\\"The supplement is a minimum grounded scope, not an authoritative posthoc ranking. Route later appendix-ranked analyses there without retroactively changing this strategy, and keep evidence essential to the central thesis in the main paper. Proposed missing studies, such as matched tuning ablations, noise stress tests, hidden endpoint twins, and inference calibration, belong in limitations/future work and must never be written as completed results.\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n+}\n*** Add File: /workspace/paper/paper_strategy.md\n+# Paper Strategy\n+\n+**Status:** ready for a bounded standalone comparison study, not a declaration of publication readiness.  \n+**Prompt family:** \"'`paper_standalone`; downstream skill: `write-paper-standalone`.  \n+**Provisional title:** Endpoint-Tuned Regression under Network Interference: Accuracy and Limits.\n+\n+## Central Question and Thesis\n+\n+**Question.** How accurately does endpoint-risk-tuned one-hop regression estimate the global average treatment effect across the evaluated networks, interference mechanisms, and assignment designs, relative to simple regression baselines?\n+\n+**Thesis.** In the evaluated synthetic-outcome settings, endpoint-tuned exposure regression reduces error relative to assignment-only comparators in many spillover conditions but performs worse in aggregate than linear one-hop OLS, while near-noise-level in-sample residuals coexist with substantial endpoint errors under threshold and omitted two-hop interference.\n+\n+This is one shared thesis with condition-specific evidence across SBM, BA, WS, and Stanford3, not four separate experiment narratives. The contribution is an implemented target-aware regression construction and an honest map of its conditional accuracy and limits. It is not a new identification theorem, proof that target-aware tuning improves estimation, or demonstration of general superiority.\n+\n+## Why This Story Wins\n+\n+The completed final evaluation provides matched estimates for the frozen method and three substantive baselines. `final.evidence.episodes` contains 24 seed blocks, covering 576 scientific cases across four graph families and six conditions. This evidence supports a direct performance-and-limitations paper even though the stronger exposure-aware baseline is better in aggregate.\n+\n+Descriptive reaggregation performed during framing gives equal-cell mean uncapped RMSE of approximately 0.6140 for the selected estimator, 0.4598 for one-hop OLS, and 1.3676 for difference in means. The selected estimator'\"'s authoritative recorded mean capped normalized RMSE is 0.6140475146408374; its mean absolute capped normalized bias is 0.4597691498768297. The reaggregated baseline values come from existing final observations, not new simulations or uncertainty estimates. The writer must save the derivation and ground the resulting table before publishing rounded values, and verify clipping before equating raw and capped summaries.\n+\n+The following alternatives are rejected:\n+\n+- **Positive development-improvement story:** a development score does not establish final superiority, and the final one-hop OLS comparison contradicts a general-benefit claim.\n+- **Broad method/architecture family:** no usable final comparison supports a neural or two-hop estimator family, and component ablations are absent.\n+- **Support or tuning as a proven causal explanation:** measured support and fit are descriptive; feature changes and tuning are not isolated.\n+- **Search chronology:** does not answer the scientific accuracy question and would conceal the strongest comparisons behind development events.\n+\n+Negative and conditional findings are the reason to write this paper, not a reason to omit the method or manufacture an improvement.\n+\n+## Framing and Input Audit\n+\n+**Decision: recentered.** This is not an edit-mode manuscript revision. No resolved edit-context input, prior manuscript, \"'`aggregated/`, `extra_tasks/`, or contributions scaffold was supplied. The label denotes recentering the source task'\"'s broad research agenda on the narrower final evidence, not revising an imaginary prior paper. Preservation is therefore inapplicable; broadening lacks evidence; an evidence-led comparison is the defensible choice.\n+\n+The inspected selected source is \"'`results/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1/src/program.py`, located through `paper_data.best_correct` and `results/best_program_manifest.json`. It implements one-hop quadratic exposure regression and endpoint-risk ridge tuning, not a two-hop model. The original source at `task/program_0/src/program.py` uses fixed hinges and fixed ridge. The final manifest identifies the frozen program tree, but an independent file-by-file identity check is not claimed here.\n+\n+`paper_data.json.final_evaluation` was read independently of study metadata. The grounded values are:\n+\n+- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n+- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n+- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n+\n+These are reported task-specific completion/integrity facts, not evidence of superiority, generalization, model validity in every setting, or publication acceptance. Missing gate diagnostics remain unknown. If later authoritative evidence reports failed qualification, retain that outcome and its stated limitations through drafting, review, and revision; do not infer its cause from performance metrics or reinterpret publication eligibility as scientific qualification.\n+\n+No `downstream_study` or `study.*` sources exist. Do not invent them or report campaign winner margins, search-score deltas, or scored/unscored attempt counts. **Private-only provenance:** development pointers locate the selected implementation; they are not main-paper performance evidence.\n+\n+The original fixed-hinge estimator lacks a matched final comparison with the frozen method. Omit that final contrast rather than comparing development and final scores. A failed two-hop GCV branch has no usable performance evidence: its local stderr reports disconnection before candidate responses, while the underlying cause remains unknown. Mention missing evidence nonchronologically if needed; never call it an estimator loss or an invalid scientific method.\n+\n+## Methods to Include\n+\n+| Method group | Role | Scientific purpose |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| Endpoint-risk-tuned quadratic one-hop regression | Proposed | The frozen evaluated construction and its guarded endpoint objective |\n+| Difference in means | Baseline | What a direct assignment contrast misses under interference; retain its favorable no-spillover evidence |\n+| Own-treatment OLS with covariates | Baseline | Ordinary adjustment without explicit exposure representation |\n+| Linear one-hop OLS with covariates | Baseline | Essential stronger comparison; it is better in aggregate and is not a same-feature tuning ablation |\n+\n+Do not expand the main comparison with copy-observed or constant-prediction behavioral checks. The original fixed-hinge method and incomplete two-hop branch belong only in implementation provenance/missing-evidence discussion, without an unsupported final comparison.\n+\n+### Explanation Worth the Body Space\n+\n+Define finite-population GATE as the average all-treated versus all-control potential-outcome difference. The estimator averages the corresponding fitted node predictions. Neighbor exposure is zero for isolates, so all-treated exposure is not automatically one for every unit.\n+\n+Explain the actual feature map: intercept, covariates, own treatment, neighbor treatment fraction, treatment-exposure interaction, exposure curvature, and treatment-curvature interaction. Describe saved centering/scaling, SVD, the fixed penalty grid, contrast estimability and residual-degrees-of-freedom guards, fixed-hinge fallback, and constant-outcome bypass. Their implementation does not establish how frequently the guards or penalties were used.\n+\n+The implemented risk criterion for a fixed penalty is\n+\n+`(a_lambda''^T y_centered - a_0''^T y_centered)''^2 + 2 sigma_hat''^2 a_lambda''^T a_0 - sigma_hat''^2 ||a_0||''^2`,\n+\n+where `a_0` represents the estimable reference contrast. Explain the correct-reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian-noise assumptions. A fixed-penalty risk identity is not unbiased risk reporting after selecting its minimum. Algebraic estimability is not endpoint support, identification, or robustness. Do not promote an unverified derivation into a new theorem.\n+\n+The candidate changes both representation and tuning relative to the original fixed-hinge regression. No same-feature predictive-GCV, fixed-penalty, or unregularized ablation has usable final evidence. The paper cannot assign gains or regressions to any single component.\n+\n+## Results That Carry the Thesis\n+\n+1. **Absolute accuracy first.** A compact main-text table reports all four methods'\"' equal-cell mean RMSE and mean absolute cell bias, with uncertainty derived from existing records. Do not substitute pooled RMSE, mean absolute error, or a composite utility for these definitions. Make the unfavorable aggregate one-hop OLS comparison explicit.\n+2. **Complete conditional comparison.** Show every final family-by-condition cell, including no spillover, weak and strong linear spillover, threshold interaction, omitted two-hop spillover, and strong linear spillover under cluster assignment. Preserve regressions and large failures in the main figures.\n+3. **Fit is not endpoint accuracy.** Plot the selected estimator's observed-fit residual RMSE against absolute GATE error. Call it in-sample residual error, not held-out prediction performance; no baseline residual measurements or predictive-GCV comparison are available.\n+4. **Bounded assignment-design contrast.** Compare strong linear spillover under individual assignment with its graph-BFS cluster counterpart within families and seed blocks. Do not infer optimal clustering or causally attribute the change to support alone.\n+5. **Supporting diagnostics.** Summarize recorded exposure ranges, endpoint fractions, and the evaluator's simple-linear-design condition measurement. The condition measurement is not that of the selected quadratic reference. Candidate internal diagnostics are unverified; penalty frequencies, fallback frequencies, risk calibration, and inference coverage are absent.\n+\n+The threshold condition combines a neighbor-exposure threshold with a treatment interaction. The two-hop condition uses length-two walks **including backtracking**, not a unique-distance-two average. The cluster condition changes assignment relative to strong linear spillover, not the outcome formula. Use the actual manifest definitions rather than a generic interference taxonomy. All outcomes, including Stanford3 outcomes, are synthetic.\n+\n+### Uncertainty and Aggregation\n+\n+Preserve the complete seed block across graph families, conditions, and methods when resampling the 24 final seed records. A planned 95% paired seed-block percentile bootstrap can summarize Monte Carlo uncertainty; document the random seed and resample count when implemented. For each resample, recompute per-cell RMSEs before forming ratios and recompute equal-cell averages before forming table intervals. No confidence intervals have yet been generated.\n+\n+The 576 cases are not 576 independent inferential replicates. The resampling quantifies Monte Carlo variation in this benchmark, not unit-level causal interval coverage. Stanford3 is one fixed public topology with repeated generated experiments, not a sample of 24 public networks. Synthetic graph variation and repeated observations on a fixed public graph have different interpretations. Avoid significance stars and posthoc win counts; per-condition comparisons are exploratory and bootstrap intervals do not resolve multiplicity.\n+\n+## Figure and Table Contract\n+\n+### Template Geometry\n+\n+\"'`VENUE.md` fixes ICLR 2026 and an 8-9-page body excluding references. The inspected `paper.sty` specifies a **single text column, 5.5 inches wide and 9 inches high**. Here column width equals full text width; there is no double-column half-width slot. Use full-width figures rather than shrinking dense panels into imagined narrow columns. The style'\"'s historical header is not independent proof of official 2026 release provenance; verify that during final preparation and regenerate assets if geometry changes.\n+\n+All figure labels must remain at least 8.5 pt at final placement. Maximum budgets are figure-specific and never exceed four panels total, three panels per row, six categories per panel, or four series per panel. Page-footprint estimates include captions and are planning allocations, not compiled measurements.\n+\n+| Figure | Content and generator | Geometry and readability | Placement / footprint |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| \"'`endpoint_overview` | Required conceptual diagram via `generate-concept-figure`; observed experiment → exposure regression and endpoint-risk tuning → mean all-treated minus all-control prediction | One cohesive panel, at most three horizontal zones, six short conceptual labels; 0.98 text width, about 5.39 × 1.7-1.9 in; no fabricated curves or numeric results | Page 1 or 2; about one quarter page |\n+| `conditional_accuracy` | Required matplotlib 2×2 dot-whisker comparison; each family has six condition rows and three ratios, selected RMSE divided by baseline RMSE | Full 5.5 in width, about 3.8-4.2 in high; two panels per row; six categories, three series per panel; shared log ratio axis and line at one; shared three-entry legend | Main results; roughly half page |\n+| `diagnostic_limits` | Required matplotlib composite: residual-fit/error scatter plus selected-estimator individual-versus-cluster RMSE | Full width, about 2.4-2.7 in high; two panels in one row. Scatter groups threshold, two-hop, and remaining conditions into three visual series; design panel has four families and two series | Main results; about one third page |\n+\n+For Figure 2, values below one favor the selected method; use ratios of cell RMSEs, never averages of per-run ratios. Intervals are planned reanalyses of recorded data. Keep a main-text table of absolute errors beside the relative comparison so ratios cannot hide poor absolute accuracy. Define the six short condition labels in a compact protocol table.\n+\n+For Figure 3, use transparent scatter points and no fitted causal trend. Correlated case records are visual observations, not extra independent replications. The assignment-design panel uses paired-seed uncertainty. Keep support-fraction tables in the supplement. If two panels cannot remain legible, stack them and adjust the body allocation rather than reduce type size.\n+\n+The overview is a semantic schematic, not an empirical network sample. Endpoint assignments are prediction inputs; endpoint outcomes are withheld from fitting. Use `generate-concept-figure` for exactly this one diagram and preserve its editable draw.io source. This stage only plans figures and does not invoke generation.\n+\n+### Export Requirements\n+\n+- Quantitative source scripts export both PDF and PNG, with the PNG at 300 DPI. LaTeX references the PDF whenever available.\n+- For the concept figure, request a vector PDF export from editable source if supported. If raster-only, regenerate at least 2400 px wide for full-width placement.\n+- Any raster assigned to a genuinely narrow column in a later changed layout needs at least 1200 px width. Never upscale existing raster output.\n+- If the concept workflow is impossible or fails, record the concrete failure before a documented vector fallback. Do not silently replace the required workflow with an improvised drawing.\n+- Plot scripts and figure assets do not yet exist; do not describe them as completed deliverables.\n+\n+## Body-Page Budget\n+\n+Target approximately **8.75 substantive pages**, including figures and tables:\n+\n+| Material | Page allocation |\n+| --- | ---: |\n+| Title and abstract | 0.40 |\n+| Introduction and early overview | 1.10 |\n+| Related work | 0.65 |\n+| Target and interference setup | 0.90 |\n+| Endpoint-tuned estimator | 1.50 |\n+| Empirical protocol and condition table | 0.95 |\n+| Conditional comparisons and diagnostic figures | 2.15 |\n+| Discussion and limitations | 0.85 |\n+| Conclusion | 0.25 |\n+\n+Use spare space for the endpoint objective, baseline definitions, conditional-risk assumptions, uncertainty/aggregation, and concrete failures. Do not add methods or studies to fill pages. Keep the main body within nine pages; an eight-page version must have its last page at least 75% full. Figures'\"' footprints are included in the relevant section allocations, not extra pages.\n+\n+Suggested thesis-first headings are **Global Intervention Target**, **Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression**, **Empirical Protocol**, **Conditional Accuracy and Failure**, and **Scope and Limitations**. The abstract and conclusion must retain the negative aggregate comparison and the lack of isolated tuning evidence.\n+\n+## Supplementary Information After References\n+\n+The supplement adds grounded depth without hiding evidence essential to the thesis. It is a minimum plan, not an authoritative ranking of later posthoc analyses.\n+\n+1. **Complete Conditional Error Tables.** All methods and family-by-condition cells: signed bias, absolute bias, uncapped RMSE, replicate counts, and paired-seed uncertainty. Explain equal-cell versus pooled summaries. Sources: \"'`final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.mean_capped_normalized_rmse`, `final.metric.mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias`.\n+2. **Exposure Support and Residual Diagnostics.** Existing minima/maxima, endpoint fractions, simple-linear-design condition measurements, and in-sample residuals. Interpret descriptively; no identifiability, calibration, or coverage claim. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`.\n+3. **Estimator Specification and Conditional Risk Criterion.** Document the inspected selected implementation, tolerances, centered/scaled SVD, penalty grid `n × {0, 1e-6, 1e-4, 1e-2, 1}`, tie handling, fallback, and constant bypass. Contrast fixed-penalty reasoning with selected-minimum optimism. `result.best_correct_score` links to the selected source directory; `final.evidence.episodes` supplies evaluated results, not independent source-hash verification. Do not invent tuning or fallback distributions.\n+4. **Benchmark Construction and Reproduction.** Use `task/resources/final/manifest.json`, `task/task_contract.json`, `task/requirements.txt`, and `task/evaluate_final.py` alongside `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.status/outcome`. Specify graph and outcome generation, the actual BFS cluster rule, backtracking two-hop exposure, noise/covariates, isolates, seeds, checksums, declared dependencies, and commands verified against existing entrypoints. Separate runtime limits from measured elapsed time. Map eventual tables and figures to scripts only after those scripts exist. Preserve Stanford3 attribution and share-alike obligations.\n+5. **Evaluation Integrity and Missing Evidence.** Use `final.qualification.*`, the recorded control/failure metrics, and final episode evidence. Explain constant-outcome, relabeling, and replay checks only to their documented scope; internal diagnostics remain unverified. A nonchronological missing-evidence note may cite the two-hop branch'\"'s local failure JSON and stderr without converting an unscored attempt into a scientific loss. No unsupported search counts, margins, or matched original-method final results.\n+6. **LLM Usage.** A separate venue-required section disclosing the precise documented roles of LLMs in ideation, code generation, query planning, framing, and eventual writing/figure work. Use actual process artifacts under \"'`results/ideas/` and local planning records, not performance source IDs or assumptions. Disclose performed activity separately from work still planned, without identifying authors.\n+\n+No ablation study, noise stress test, hidden-endpoint twin simulation, risk-calibration study, or inference-coverage experiment is available. Such work may be named as missing or future work, never as supplementary results already obtained. Route later appendix-ranked analyses here without retroactively rewriting this strategy; keep essential negative findings in the main body.\n+\n+## Citation, Access, and Venue Guardrails\n+\n+`references.bib` includes relevant starter-title matches `Chin2018RegressionAF` and `Han2023ModelbasedRA`, but the supplied reference provenance records retrieval metadata rather than verified reading. Other retrieved statistical titles are not automatically appropriate foundations for the implemented risk criterion. Use only existing keys after checking and reading permitted sources; check questionable year metadata. Do not invent graph-attribution keys or pad the related work with irrelevant hits. Missing literature acquisition belongs to an authorized reference step.\n+\n+Do not identify or search for the prohibited paper, expand citation graphs, or read prohibited summaries, reproductions, or discussions. If accidental exposure occurs, stop and record the location and extent. This strategy stage performed no external research and no new scientific runs.\n+\n+The ICLR 2026 manuscript must be fully anonymous: no names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, self-identifying links, or internal URLs. Include the separate `LLM Usage` section after references if appropriate. Current readiness applies to framing only, not verified official-template provenance, citation completeness, completed plot assets, uncertainty computations, or compiled-page compliance.\n+\n+## Language That Would Break the Story\n+\n+**Forbidden headings:** Search Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; Timeline of Improvements; First Successful Estimator; Experiment A / Experiment B; From Baseline to Winner; Architecture Sweep; and an Ablation Results heading without actual component ablations.\n+\n+**Forbidden visible phrases:** “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “next-best,” “later variants,” “search winner proves superiority,” “universally outperforms,” “state of the art,” “robust to arbitrary interference,” “validated endpoint-risk calibration,” “unbiased after tuning,” “predictive GCV is inferior,” and “the two-hop method failed scientifically.”\n+\n+Keep internal labels such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `combined_score`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, and `rubric` out of visible manuscript prose. Do not use a favorable composite utility as GATE accuracy. No thresholded-rate plot is needed; if added later, define one reader-facing meaning per figure.\n+\n+## Handoff Boundary\n+\n+This stage writes only the mirrored strategy outputs and append-only memory updates. It does not draft `paper.tex`, create `claims_grounding.json`, generate figures, execute a new simulation, or run final-paper validation. Later writing must save derived summaries, compute uncertainty, inspect allowed sources, and preserve all stated limitations before claiming a complete paper.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\n)\"'","aggregated_output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA /workspace/paper/paper_strategy.json\nA /workspace/paper/paper_strategy.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
